<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ripon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ripon is a cathedral city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. A monastery founded there around 660 was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, who built a stone church whose seventh-century crypt survives beneath the present cathedral. Ripon was one of the centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumbrian-renaissance">Northumbrian Renaissance</a>; it was burned by the English king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadred">Eadred</a> in 948, and its minster became a cathedral in 1836.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ripon.jpg" alt="Ripon" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MatthewDavid41 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Market_Place_Ripon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ripon lies at the confluence of two tributaries of the River Ure, the Laver and the Skell, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, and was originally known as Inhrypum. The area was once under the Brigantes, who built a large circular earthwork at Hutton Moor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> did not settle at Ripon but kept a military outpost about 8 km away at North Stainley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Solid evidence for Ripon begins in the 7th century, under the Anglian kingdom of Northumbria. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhfrith">Alhfrith</a> of Deira gave land to Eata of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexham">Hexham</a> for a monastery, with monks including the young Cuthbert as guest-master. When Alhfrith drove Eata out, the abbey passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, who replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> church with one of stone, using stonemasons, glaziers and plasterers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 937 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Athelstan</a> granted the church sanctuary for a mile around, and after the Northumbrians rebelled in 948 King Edred had the buildings burned. Prosperity returned by the end of the 10th century, when the body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a> was moved to Ripon for a time because of the threat of Danish raids.</p><p>After the Harrying of the North that followed the rebellion of 1069, Ripon is thought to have shrunk to a small community around the church, and a Gothic Collegiate Church was built over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>'s building. In the 12th century a wool trade drew Florentine merchants, and after 1326 a cloth industry grew that was the third largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> after York and Halifax. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> badly affected the town, and 300 rebels of the Rising of the North were hanged at Gallows Hill in January 1570. In the 16th and 17th centuries Ripon was known for its spurs, which gave rise to the proverb as true <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> as Ripon Rowels. A treaty ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a> was signed there in 1640.</p><p>In 1836 Ripon's church became a cathedral when the diocese was created, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. The council presumed this made the town a city, and the City of Ripon Act of 1865 recognised the status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cathedral, an English Gothic building, has been rebuilt many times, and only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> crypt is original. It holds the grave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>. The Ripon Hornblower tradition is said to date from 886, and the Ripon Obelisk in the market square was erected in 1702. Nearby are Studley Royal Park and Fountains Abbey, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ripon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The monasteries of Lindisfarne, Wearmouth-Jarrow, Whitby, Hexham and <b>Ripon</b> created the so-called Northumbrian Renaissance, which blended Irish, Anglo-Saxon and Mediterranean traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting in a schoolhouse in <b>Ripon</b>, Wisconsin, opponents of the Kansas-Nebraska Act called for a new party.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-20">20 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
